.TH CJSON_LIST_PUSH 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
cjson_list_push \- append a child to an array node
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <cjson.h>

ccol_retval_t cjson_list_push(cjson arr, cjson child);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Appends child to the end of arr.  Ownership of child transfers to arr
on every outcome that touches child at all: on success the array owns
child; on a failure caused by arr being invalid (NULL or not
CJSON_LIST) or by an internal insertion failure, a freshly unattached
child is deep-freed by cjson_list_push and must not be freed by the
caller.  The one class of failure that leaves child completely
untouched, still owned by whatever it was already attached to (or
still a free-standing tree of its own), is described next; this holds
regardless of what else is wrong with the call, so an invalid arr
never causes an already-attached child to be destroyed.

child must not already be attached to a list/dictionary parent, and
must not already contain arr somewhere within its own subtree.  This
call fails with ccol_invalid_args, leaving child completely untouched,
if child is already a child of arr or of any other list/dictionary
node, if child is arr itself, or if arr is already reachable by
descending from child (attaching child would then make arr both a new
ancestor of child and an existing descendant of it, a cycle).  If that
last check cannot be completed under memory pressure,
ccol_not_enough_memory is returned instead of silently risking an
undetected cycle.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
arr    Target list node (must be CJSON_LIST).
child  Node to append; must not already be attached elsewhere, and
       must not already contain arr in its own subtree.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success, ccol_invalid_args, ccol_not_enough_memory, or
ccol_container_full if arr has already reached its maximum element
count.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
A node returned by cjson_get(), cjson_list_get(), or
cjson_dictionary_get() is a borrowed reference into an existing tree,
not a freshly owned node, and must never be passed as child directly.
Use cjson_clone() first to obtain an independent copy that is safe to
push.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cjson_list_get (3),
.BR cjson_create_list (3),
.BR cjson_clone (3)
